WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.660
In the first film, duplication multiplied animal receptors over a shared core.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.960
How does a copied receptor plug into a new pathway without inventing a new enzyme?

3
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.760
Start with a simpler design.

4
00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:20.723
Many bacteria use a two-component relay.

5
00:00:22.023 --> 00:00:27.243
A membrane sensor kinase takes one phosphate from ATP and puts it on its own

6
00:00:27.243 --> 00:00:32.883
histidine. Its partner responder moves that phosphate onto its own aspartate,

7
00:00:33.363 --> 00:00:35.263
flipping its shape to switch genes on.

8
00:00:36.383 --> 00:00:41.903
The sensor's enzyme has a different fold from animal kinases, and animals lack this

9
00:00:41.903 --> 00:00:42.903
system.

10
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:47.973
Notice that mark. A phosphate on aspartate is short-lived.

11
00:00:49.193 --> 00:00:53.993
The responder itself helps shed it, within seconds for some, minutes or more for

12
00:00:53.993 --> 00:00:57.673
others. It changes only that responder.

13
00:00:58.653 --> 00:01:04.013
On an animal receptor tail, a phosphate on tyrosine stays until a phosphatase

14
00:01:04.013 --> 00:01:08.733
removes it. What new kind of connection does a lasting mark allow?

15
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:18.077
Because the mark stays put, it acts as a docking tag.

16
00:01:19.477 --> 00:01:24.697
Separate reader pieces, like SH2 domains, bind the marked tyrosine and recruit

17
00:01:24.697 --> 00:01:25.897
downstream pathways.

18
00:01:27.317 --> 00:01:31.537
One receptor can hold several marks and launch several branches at once.

19
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.660
Modular parts make rewiring cheap in three ways.

20
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.620
A domain is a folded piece of protein that works on its own.

21
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.460
First, moving an SH2 domain onto another protein builds a new link in one step.

22
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:54.320
Second, mutating one or two amino acids beside a tyrosine builds a new docking site.

23
00:01:55.283 --> 00:02:01.363
Third, regulatory DNA is a short stretch, usually near a gene, that decides which

24
00:02:01.363 --> 00:02:07.183
cells build it. Changing it puts an unchanged receptor into a new tissue with

25
00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:08.183
different targets.

26
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:15.053
Genome counts match: budding yeast has one SH2 protein, while humans carry over a

27
00:02:15.053 --> 00:02:16.053
hundred.

28
00:02:16.483 --> 00:02:18.703
When did this tyrosine toolkit appear?

29
00:02:19.963 --> 00:02:24.603
Look at a choanoflagellate, the closest living single-celled relative of animals.

30
00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:32.883
It carries 128 tyrosine kinases to our 90, plus tyrosine phosphatases and SH2

31
00:02:32.883 --> 00:02:38.763
domains. Yet it lacks recognisable genes for pathways like TGF-beta.

32
00:02:40.203 --> 00:02:44.503
Parts shared by animals and their closest relative were likely in their common

33
00:02:44.503 --> 00:02:47.243
ancestor, so the kit predates animals.

34
00:02:48.643 --> 00:02:50.843
Its job in one cell is largely unknown.

35
00:02:51.967 --> 00:02:57.327
Rewiring copied receptors into shared hubs builds a network rather than separate

36
00:02:57.327 --> 00:03:03.867
wires. If one receptor fails, sisters made in the same cells can cover part of the

37
00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:08.667
loss. Hubs buy robustness, but they bring clinical costs too.

38
00:03:10.087 --> 00:03:14.827
Modular domains, short motifs, and regulatory DNA make new wiring cheap.
